## Why Ecommerce Sites Need Specialist SEO

Ecommerce websites are fundamentally different from blogs, brochure sites, or SaaS platforms. A typical online store might have thousands of product pages, dozens of category and subcategory pages, filtered navigation generating hundreds of URL parameters, and seasonal inventory changes that create constant indexing challenges.

Generic SEO advice rarely accounts for these complexities. An ecommerce SEO consultant understands the interplay between crawl budget, thin content, duplicate pages, and the commercial intent behind every search query. The difference between ranking a blog post and ranking a product category page for a high-intent keyword like "buy running shoes online" requires entirely different technical and content strategies.

Online sales now run at over 28% of total UK retail, with mobile carrying the majority of ecommerce traffic. Against that backdrop, the platform-specific technical debt covered in my [UK ecommerce statistics](/blog/uk-ecommerce-seo-statistics/) breakdown, duplicate collection URLs, thin category pages, slow product pages, is exactly what separates stores that convert their organic traffic from stores that rank without ever seeing the revenue.

I run [technical SEO audits](/services/technical-seo-audit/) with ecommerce platforms in mind. They identify the issues that cost you revenue, not a generic crawl-tool warning list.

## Common Ecommerce SEO Problems I Solve

Online stores share a predictable set of issues. Here are the ones I check for first:

**Duplicate content from faceted navigation.** Filters for colour, size, price range, and brand generate thousands of near-identical URLs. Without proper canonicalisation, parameter handling, and crawl directives, search engines waste crawl budget on pages that should never be indexed.

**Thin or missing category page content.** Category pages are your most powerful ranking assets for high-volume commercial keywords. Yet most stores leave them as bare product grids with no supporting copy, no internal links, and no structured data.

**Poor product schema implementation.** Product structured data drives rich results in Google: price, availability, reviews, and ratings displayed directly in search listings. Incorrect or incomplete schema means you lose click-through rate to competitors who implement it properly.

**Cannibalisation between product and category pages.** When your product pages compete with your category pages for the same keywords, neither ranks well. Resolving this requires deliberate [keyword research](/services/keyword-research/) and a clear content hierarchy.

**Orphaned products and broken internal linking.** Products removed from navigation, out-of-stock items returning 404 errors, and weak [internal linking](/services/internal-linking/) structures all erode your site's ability to pass authority where it matters most.

**Slow page speed on product pages.** High-resolution product images, third-party review widgets, and bloated theme code create performance issues that directly impact rankings and conversion rates.

## How a UK ecommerce brand should evaluate an AEO or AI visibility agency

**Ask any AEO agency or consultant how they measure AI visibility, what they change on your product data and what they will not promise. A good answer names a fixed set of buyer prompts checked every month. It also covers work on your product feed, Merchant Center data, product pages and reviews. A promise of ChatGPT inclusion is a red flag.**

AI answers vary from one run to the next. Nobody can guarantee your products appear. Use the questions below on every shortlist. That includes me.

| Question to ask | Good answer | Red flag |
|---|---|---|
| How will you measure AI visibility? | A fixed panel of buyer prompts run in the same assistants each month with results and sources logged | Screenshots of one good answer or a single "AI score" with no method |
| Can you guarantee we appear in ChatGPT or Google AI Overviews? | No. They can show what changed and how often you appeared | Guaranteed inclusion or a guaranteed position |
| What will you do with our product feed and Merchant Center data? | Check titles, attributes, prices, availability and GTINs match the product pages | Product data is not mentioned at all |
| How do you handle structured product data? | Make Product markup accurate and consistent with the page and the feed as basic hygiene | Selling schema or llms.txt as a way to get cited |
| What about reviews? | Help you collect genuine reviews and show them on product pages and trusted review platforms | Buying reviews or offering incentives in exchange for positive ones |
| Which sources do AI answers cite for our products? | A list of the retailers, review sites and publishers cited today plus a plan for the ones you can influence | No research into cited sources |
| How do you report sales and enquiries? | AI referral sessions, orders and enquiries reported separately with the numbers behind each | Only "mentions" or "visibility" with no link to revenue |
| What does it cost and what is excluded? | A fixed price for the audit and a clear monthly scope | Long lock-in contracts with vague deliverables |

My [AI visibility audit](/ai-visibility/) is a £1,500 fixed fee. It checks whether your store is mentioned, cited or recommended for an agreed set of buyer questions. It includes a competitor benchmark and a prioritised 90-day plan. Delivery takes 2 weeks.

## Platform-Specific Ecommerce SEO

### Shopify SEO Consultant

Shopify is brilliant for getting a store live quickly, but its rigid URL structure and limited technical control create specific SEO challenges.

As a [Shopify SEO consultant](/services/shopify-seo-consultant/), I work within the platform's constraints to maximise organic visibility.

Key Shopify SEO issues I address include the enforced `/collections/` and `/products/` URL structure, duplicate content from product-in-collection URLs, limited control over robots.txt (though Shopify now allows customisation via the theme file), automatic pagination handling, and theme-level performance optimisation. I also advise on which Shopify apps genuinely help SEO versus which ones add unnecessary bloat.

### WooCommerce SEO Consultant

WooCommerce offers far more technical flexibility than Shopify, but that flexibility is a double-edged sword. As a WooCommerce SEO consultant, I help store owners configure their WordPress and WooCommerce settings correctly from the ground up, or fix the mess left by poorly configured plugins.

Common WooCommerce SEO work includes optimising permalink structures, configuring Yoast or Rank Math for product and category pages, managing attribute archives and tag pages that generate thin content, implementing proper product schema beyond what plugins generate by default, and resolving conflicts between caching plugins, CDN configuration, and dynamic pricing.

### Magento SEO Consultant

Magento (now Adobe Commerce) powers some of the largest ecommerce operations in the UK. I work with Magento stores that need SEO across large catalogues.

Magento-specific SEO challenges include layered navigation generating massive numbers of indexable URLs, configurable and grouped product pages creating duplicate content, complex multi-store and multi-language setups requiring proper hreflang implementation, and performance optimisation on a platform known for being resource-heavy. I also work with Magento's built-in canonical URL settings and meta robot configurations to ensure clean indexation across the entire catalogue.

## What My Ecommerce SEO Service Includes

Every engagement starts with a comprehensive technical SEO audit of your store, followed by a prioritised action plan based on revenue impact. Here is what I typically deliver:

**Technical SEO foundations.** Crawl budget optimisation, canonicalisation strategy, XML sitemap configuration for products and categories, robots.txt refinement, Core Web Vitals improvements, and structured data implementation (Product, BreadcrumbList, FAQPage, Organization).

**Category page strategy.** [Keyword research](/services/keyword-research/) to identify the highest-value commercial terms, content briefs for category pages, [on-page SEO](/services/on-page-seo/) optimisation, and internal linking architecture that channels authority to your most important pages.

**Product page optimisation.** Unique product descriptions, proper schema markup, image optimisation, and cross-linking between related products and relevant category pages.

**Content strategy for [topical authority](/services/topical-authority/).** Ecommerce stores that invest in supporting content such as buying guides, comparison pages, and how-to articles build topical authority that lifts the entire domain. I create content strategies that directly support your product and category rankings.

**Ongoing [SEO consulting](/services/seo-consulting/).** Monthly reporting, priority adjustments based on performance data, and strategic guidance as your catalogue evolves.

